// movies.ontology.gram
//
// SPIKE. Gram translation of movies.yaml, the example in neo4j/ontology-graph-spec
// (schema 1.0.0). Source copy: ./movies.yaml. Builds on ../graphtype/ conventions.
//
// STANCE
// ======
// Qualification is external. A node does not know its context, so nothing is
// nested inside an element to describe it. Meaning (description, aliases,
// reference) is a separate `Meaning` pattern attached with `@on(...)`, and a
// `Glossary` context groups the meanings that apply together. The same element
// can have other Meaning patterns in other Glossary contexts (another audience
// or language) without being edited.
//
// MAPPING (spec -> gram)
// ======================
//   schema (URL)            header record `schema`
//   id                      identity of the Ontology subject pattern
//   name, version           record on the Ontology pattern
//   description, aliases,   a Meaning pattern, `@on(<element>)`, and for a
//   reference               property also `@property(<key>)`. Grouped by Glossary.
//   nodes.<id>              (<id>::<Label> { ... }). The spec id is the pattern
//                           identity; labels.identifier (or the `label`
//                           shorthand) is the `::` label. Implied labels would
//                           follow as `:Implied`.
//   properties.<key>        record entry `key:: cypher`TYPE``.
//                           must_exist: true  -> `TYPE!`
//                           unique / key      -> @unique(key) / @key(key)
//   relationships.<id>      (<from>)=[<id>::<TYPE> { ... }]=>(<to>), where
//                           from.node and to.node are node identities.
//
// NOT COVERED by this file (not in movies.yaml): longhand named constraints,
// indexes, display, custom, data sources and mappings, relationship counts,
// one_of, pattern, dimension, optional labels, property `name`.
//
// DIFFERENCES FROM ../graphtype/ (deliberate, to review)
//   1. Identity is the spec id (`person`), not the label (`Person`), so
//      references and round-tripping survive two nodes sharing a label.
//   2. Fat arrow is written `=[...]=>`, which is what the tree-sitter-gram
//      grammar defines. The graphtype files write `==[...]=>`.
//
// OPEN QUESTIONS
//   - `@property(name)` addresses a property of an element, following the
//     `@on(Animal) @property(id)` precedent in graphtype.schema.gram. Properties
//     have no identity of their own, so nothing checks the key exists.
//   - `STRING!` folds the spec's EXISTS constraint into the type string, as the
//     graphtype files do. The spec keeps type and constraint separate.
//   - Glossary is this spike's name for a meaning context. The spec has no
//     such concept.

{
  kind: "ontology",
  schema: url`https://neo4j.com/docs/ontology-graph-spec/1.0.0/schema.json`
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NODE ELEMENT TYPES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

(person::Person {
  name:: cypher`STRING!`
})

@unique(title)
(movie::Movie {
  title:: cypher`STRING`
})


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RELATIONSHIP ELEMENT TYPES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

(person)=[acted-in::ACTED_IN {
  role:: cypher`STRING`
}]=>(movie)


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONTOLOGY
//
// Names its members by identity, as the GraphType pattern does.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

[movies::Ontology {
  name:    "Movies",
  version: "1.0.0"
} |
  person, movie,
  acted-in
]


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEANING
//
// description, aliases and reference live outside the elements they describe.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@on(movies)
[movies-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "People and the movies they act in."
}]

@on(person)
[person-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "A person who acts in movies.",
  aliases:     ["Actor", "Performer"]
}]

@on(person) @property(name)
[person-name-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "The person's full name."
}]

@on(movie)
[movie-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "A film released to the public.",
  aliases:     ["Film"]
}]

@on(movie) @property(title)
[movie-title-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "The title the movie was released under."
}]

@on(acted-in)
[acted-in-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "The person appeared in the movie as a cast member."
}]

@on(acted-in) @property(role)
[acted-in-role-meaning::Meaning {
  description: "The character the person played."
}]


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONTEXT
//
// The meanings that apply together for this ontology.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@on(movies)
[movies-glossary::Glossary |
  movies-meaning,
  person-meaning, person-name-meaning,
  movie-meaning, movie-title-meaning,
  acted-in-meaning, acted-in-role-meaning
]
